==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro}} {{term|maistrie|lang=fr}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|masteries}}

# The [[position]] or [[authority]] of a [[master]]; [[dominion]]; [[command]]; [[supremacy]]; [[superiority]].
#: ''If divided by mountains, they will fight for the '''mastery''' of the passages of the tops.'' &mdash; Sir Walter Raleigh.
# Superiority in war or competition; [[victory]]; [[triumph]]; [[preeminence]].
#: ''The voice of them that shout for '''mastery'''.'' &mdash; Exodus. xxxii. 18.
#: ''Every man that striveth for the '''mastery''' is temperate in all things.'' &mdash; 1 Corinthians. ix. 25.
#: ''O, but to have gulled him / Had been a '''mastery'''.'' &mdash; Ben Jonson.
# {{obsolete}} [[contest|Contest]] for [[superiority]].
#: {{rfquotek|Holland}}
# {{obsolete}} A [[masterly]] operation; a [[feat]].
#: ''I will do a maistrie ere I go.'' Chaucer.
# {{obsolete}} The [[philosopher's stone]].
# The act or process of mastering; the state of having mastered; [[expertise]].
#: ''He could attain to a '''mastery''' in all languages.'' &mdash; Tillotson.
#: ''The learning and '''mastery''' of a tongue, being unpleasant in itself, should not be cumbered with other difficulties.'' &mdash; Locke.

